vscode: select the block, Command Palette, "Transform to Uppercase"
open: flags.md

@brief
Every operator you know works on a selection, and so do a few keys that
are only operators when there is a selection to work on. `~` toggles the
case of the whole region, `u` lowercases it, `U` uppercases it, `J` joins
its lines. In normal mode `~` and `J` only ever touch one character or
one line at a time; with a selection they take the lot.

The feature-flag names in the notes were written in lowercase and the
config loader only reads upper case. Uppercase the four flag names. The
rest of the file, including the heading and the sentence above them,
stays as it is.

@keys
| `U` | uppercase the selection |
| `u` | lowercase the selection |
| `~` | toggle the case of the selection |
| `J` | join the selected lines |
| `ip` | in visual mode, select the paragraph — like `vip` |

@hints
The four names are one paragraph: a block of lines with a blank line
either side. Text objects work in visual mode too.
--
Get onto any of the four lines, press `v`, then `ip`. The whole
paragraph lights up. Now the operator.
--
`/refund` `Enter` lands on the first flag. `vipU`.

@solution
    /refund<Enter> v i p U :wq<Enter>

Sixteen keystrokes. `gUip` from normal mode does the same job in the
same count, so the point is not this one edit. It is that once a region
is selected, `U`, `u`, `~` and `J` become whole-region commands. `J`
especially: `VjjjJ` joins exactly the lines you can see highlighted,
which is far harder to get wrong than counting for `4J`.

Notice the text object. `ip` after `v` selected the paragraph the same
way `dip` would have deleted it. Every text object from chapter 10 —
`iw`, `i(`, `a"`, `it` — works after `v`, and lesson 6 shows the one
thing they can do in visual mode that they cannot do with an operator.

@start flags.md
# Feature flags

Flags are read from the environment by the config loader and must be
written in upper case. The current set:

refund_window_days
refund_auto_approve
order_cutoff_hour
payments_retry_limit

Unset flags fall back to the defaults in `config.default.ts`.

@expected flags.md
# Feature flags

Flags are read from the environment by the config loader and must be
written in upper case. The current set:

REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS
REFUND_AUTO_APPROVE
ORDER_CUTOFF_HOUR
PAYMENTS_RETRY_LIMIT

Unset flags fall back to the defaults in `config.default.ts`.
